  "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14263803/assets/fbf6ef1ca91f998ab4dc2dda1890ffc8a713f2eeff19e5569351b2466256d63a.png)\n\nFrom the graph, we note the following:\n\n- The period of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$\n\n- Our graph is symmetric about the $y$-axis. This means that $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is an *even* function, and we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x)=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x.\n$$\n\nLet's now evaluate our expression:\n\n- Firstly, using the periodicity of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x-10\\pi ) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x-2\\pi \\cdot 5) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Secondly, using the evenness of $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x) & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡x \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sec}⁡(-x-10\\pi )-5 & =3(3)-5 \\\\  & =9-5 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
